All 8 inline comments posted successfully to PR #11.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "main.py",
    "line": 24,
    "summary": "create_app() unconditionally calls create_db_and_tables() at import time against the default dev engine, bypassing the get_session dependency entirely.",
    "failure_scenario": "Running pytest triggers tests/conftest.py's client fixture (`from main import app`) before/independent of the dependency override; every test run writes a real mission_control.db to the working directory (empirically confirmed), risking 'database is locked' under parallel workers and failing outright on a read-only CI checkout."
  },
  {
    "file": "models/database.py",
    "line": 44,
    "summary": "get_session() is hardwired to the single module-level `engine` global with no injection seam, forcing tests/conftest.py to hand-duplicate its body.",
    "failure_scenario": "Any future change to session semantics (commit/rollback handling, expire_on_commit) must be manually re-applied to the conftest.py copy or the test suite silently diverges from production session behavior."
  },
  {
    "file": "models/user.py",
    "line": 15,
    "summary": "`email` is globally unique rather than scoped per org_id in an explicitly multi-tenant B2B system.",
    "failure_scenario": "Two different orgs try to onboard a user with the same email (shared contractor, or both using a generic address); the second INSERT fails with a UNIQUE violation across unrelated tenants. May be intentional given FR-2's login-by-email-alone flow, but worth confirming."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_smoke.py",
    "line": 36,
    "summary": "test_all_ten_tables_created hand-queries sqlite_master and hardcodes table names instead of using SQLAlchemy's portable inspect() API.",
    "failure_scenario": "Moving off SQLite (e.g. Postgres in prod) breaks this test immediately since sqlite_master doesn't exist there; the hardcoded name list can also silently drift from the real model set."
  },
  {
    "file": "models/database.py",
    "line": 19,
    "summary": "get_database_url()'s docstring claims tests use it for a temp SQLite file, but tests/conftest.py bypasses it entirely, passing an explicit URL to make_engine().",
    "failure_scenario": "A maintainer sets MISSION_CONTROL_DATABASE_URL expecting the test suite to honor it and is confused when tests silently ignore it."
  },
  {
    "file": "README.md",
    "line": 1,
    "summary": "Unsolicited README.md added in T1, though the PRD explicitly assigns the README to T9 and T1's acceptance criteria list no README item.",
    "failure_scenario": "Violates the user's global CLAUDE.md rule: 'Prefer editing existing files over creating new ones. No unsolicited README or docs files' / 'Keep changes scoped to the request.'"
  },
  {
    "file": "models/__init__.py",
    "line": 24,
    "summary": "Entity re-exports are maintained as two separate hand-written lists (imports + __all__) that must be kept in sync by name.",
    "failure_scenario": "Adding a new entity module and forgetting to update __all__ doesn't error — it silently omits the model from `from models import Foo`, surfacing later as a confusing ImportError."
  },
  {
    "file": "models/crew_skill.py",
    "line": 18,
    "summary": "`proficiency: int` has no range constraint (docstring/FR-6 say 1-5), and AvailabilityWindow similarly lacks a start_date <= end_date check.",
    "failure_scenario": "CrewSkill(proficiency=0 or 999), or an inverted availability window, is accepted silently by SQLite with no DB-level backstop for downstream matching logic (T2+)."
  }
]
```

Notes on process: this was a pure-addition scaffold PR (710 insertions, 0 deletions across 32 new/modified files), so the removed-behavior angle correctly found nothing. The standout finding (#1) was empirically verified by one of the finder agents, which actually ran `pytest` and confirmed a real `mission_control.db` file gets written to disk as an import-time side effect — a genuine test-isolation bug despite the PR's stated goal of using a real, isolated temporary SQLite DB per test session. The README finding was checked against `docs/prd/mission-control.md`, which explicitly assigns the README to task T9, not T1.
